$FG,5$$TX+CX,"Haile Selassie I — Appeal to the League of Nations"$$FG$

$FG,2$Date:$FG$    7 June 1936
$FG,2$Venue:$FG$   League of Nations Assembly, Geneva
$FG,2$Context:$FG$ Delivered after Italy's invasion of Ethiopia and the
          use of poison gas against Ethiopian forces and civilians.
          Selassie I, Emperor of Ethiopia, addressed the international
          body that had failed to act on collective security.

------------------------------------------------------------

$FG,2$Selected passages$FG$

  $FG,3$"It is not only upon warriors that the Italian Government has
   made war."$FG$

  $FG,3$"I pray to Almighty God that He may spare nations the terrible
   sufferings that have just been inflicted on my people."$FG$

  $FG,3$"Apart from the Kingdom of the Lord there is not on this earth
   any nation that is superior to any other."$FG$

  $FG,3$"It is international morality which is at stake and not the
   Articles of the Covenant."$FG$

  $FG,3$"What reply shall I have to take back to my people?"$FG$

  $FG,3$"God and history will remember your judgment."$FG$

------------------------------------------------------------

$FG,2$Note$FG$

The phrase widely attributed to this address — "It is us today, it
will be you tomorrow" — does not appear in the documented text of
the speech. It is a paraphrase that circulated later in popular and
historical writing. ZionOS records the verified text above and notes
the famous paraphrase here without endorsing it as a direct quote.

